## Propagating Trace Context Through RabbitMQ Headers

The issue is that RabbitMQ doesn't automatically carry the W3C `traceparent`/`tracestate` headers. You need to manually inject context on publish and extract it on consume.

### Service A – Publisher (inject context)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Diagnostics.Propagation;
using RabbitMQ.Client;

// Define an ActivitySource for your publisher
static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceA.Messaging");

// When publishing:
using var activity = Source.StartActivity("OrderEvent Publish", ActivityKind.Producer);

var props = channel.CreateBasicProperties();
props.Headers ??= new Dictionary<string, object>();

// Inject current trace context into message headers
DistributedContextPropagator.Current.Inject(
    activity, props.Headers, static (headers, key, value) =>
    {
        ((IDictionary<string, object>)headers!)[key] = value;
    });

channel.BasicPublish(exchange, routingKey, props, body);
```

### Service B – Consumer (extract context)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Diagnostics.Propagation;

static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceB.Messaging");

// In your consumer callback:
var headers = ea.BasicProperties.Headers ?? new Dictionary<string, object>();

// Extract propagation fields
DistributedContextPropagator.Current.ExtractTraceIdAndState(
    headers,
    static (headers, key) =>
    {
        var dict = (IDictionary<string, object>)headers!;
        return dict.TryGetValue(key, out var val) && val is byte[] bytes
            ? System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetString(bytes)
            : null;
    },
    out var traceParent,
    out var traceState);

// Start a linked activity using the extracted parent
ActivityContext parentContext = default;
if (ActivityContext.TryParse(traceParent, traceState, out var parsed))
    parentContext = parsed;

using var activity = Source.StartActivity(
    "OrderEvent Process",
    ActivityKind.Consumer,
    parentContext);

// Now process the message — this activity is part of the same trace
```

### Wire up in `Program.cs` (both services)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(t => t
        .AddSource("ServiceA.Messaging")   // or ServiceB.Messaging
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()
        .AddOtlpExporter());
```

### Key points

| Concept | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Header format** | RabbitMQ stores header values as `byte[]`, so decode with `Encoding.UTF8.GetString` on extract |
| **ActivityKind** | Use `Producer` on publish, `Consumer` on receive — exporters use this to link spans correctly |
| **Propagator** | `DistributedContextPropagator.Current` uses W3C TraceContext by default in .NET 8 |
| **Alternative** | If you use MassTransit or NServiceBus, they do this automatically — raw `RabbitMQ.Client` requires manual propagation |

This gives you a single end-to-end trace spanning both services with the queue hop visible as a producer→consumer span pair.
